## Optionally add RoCE support on *head node*

RoCE (RDMA over Converged Ethernet) runs RDMA over Ethernet NICs that support
it, for example NVIDIA ConnectX "Dx" models. It uses the same distro-provided
RDMA stack as InfiniBand, but it needs no subnet manager and no IPoIB: RDMA
traffic is addressed by the Ethernet interface's own IP address. This recipe
runs RoCE over the internal cluster network. The following command adds the
RDMA stack to the *head node*. If you enabled InfiniBand above, the stack is
already installed and the script skips this step.

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Optionally add RoCE support on head node -->
<!-- ohpc_if_set enable_roce -->
<!-- ohpc_if [[ ${enable_ib} -ne 1 ]] -->
```bash
{{ pkg_group_install }} "InfiniBand Support"
# Load the RDMA drivers for the RoCE NIC
udevadm trigger --type=devices --action=add
systemctl restart rdma-load-modules@rdma.service
```
<!-- ohpc_fi -->
<!-- ohpc_fi -->
<!-- ohpc_end -->

RoCE takes its own MTU (up to 4096 bytes) from the Ethernet MTU: the largest
that fits with room for headers. An Ethernet MTU of 1500 gives RoCE 1024, and
exactly 4096 gives only 2048; about 4200 or more (9000 is common) gives the full
4096. RoCE works at the default MTU, and a larger one adds a little bandwidth.
Every host and switch port on the network must carry the larger frames.
{% if is_warewulf %}
To use one, set `${roce_mtu}`. The following sets it on the *head node*'s
internal interface, now and across reboots; [Enable RoCE
drivers](#enable-roce-drivers) sets the same value on the computes.

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Optionally set the RoCE MTU on head node -->
<!-- ohpc_if_set enable_roce -->
<!-- ohpc_if [[ -n "${roce_mtu}" ]] -->
```bash
# Set the Ethernet MTU on the internal interface
nmcli connection modify \
	"$(nmcli -g GENERAL.CONNECTION device show "${sms_eth_internal}")" \
	802-3-ethernet.mtu "${roce_mtu}"
ip link set dev "${sms_eth_internal}" mtu "${roce_mtu}"
```
<!-- ohpc_fi -->
<!-- ohpc_fi -->
<!-- ohpc_end -->
{% else %}
This recipe leaves the MTU unchanged. To use a larger one, set the same value
on the *head node*'s internal interface and on the computes' interfaces in your
{{ provisioner_name }} network configuration.
{% endif %}
